This directory is for containing the real 3D images.

The images in vtk format have not been uploaded because they are too big.
Here are the instructions to generate them.

A) Images from TU Wien University:
https://www.cg.tuwien.ac.at/research/vis/datasets/
Download the data and convert them to vtk format. Conversion is performed by program  dat2vtk.py,
see inside for instructions.
You get the three files
beetle.vtk
present.vtk
tree.vtk

B) Images from Stanford University:
https://graphics.stanford.edu/data/voldata/
Download the data, convert to vtk format (stage 1), and add slices along z-direction (stage 2).
The addition of a layer of voxels between any consecutive layers along
the z-direction is necessary because z-spacing was double than
x- and y-spacing (the greylevels of the inserted layers are interpolated).
The two stages are performed by the programs  decode.py (stage 1) and  addsliceZ.py (stage 2). 
See inside for instructions.
You get the three files
ctheadZ.vtk
mrbrainZ.vtk

C) After generating the images, you have to smooth them by running the program average.py (located in the parent directory)
with neighborhhod size 5x5x5. The commands are:
python3 average.py beetle.vtk" 5
python3 average.py present.vtk" 5
python3 average.py tree.vtk" 5
python3 average.py ctheadZ.vtk" 5
python3 average.py mrbrainZ.vtk" 5

You will get the same images with prefix "avg_5_", that is
avg_5_beetle.vtk
avg_5_present.vtk
avg_5_tree.vtk
avg_5_ctheadZ.vtk
avg_5_mrbrainZ.vtk
These images will be the input for generating the edge surfaces.
